## [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/how-power-outage-in-portugal-spain-impacted-internet/#impacts-in-portugal) Impacts in Portugal
### [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/how-power-outage-in-portugal-spain-impacted-internet/#country-level) Country level
In Portugal, Internet traffic dropped as the power grid failed, with traffic immediately dropping by half as compared to the previous week, falling to approximately 90% below the previous week within the next five hours.

![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%201%201%22%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%221%22%20height%3D%221%22%20fill%3D%22rgb(243%2C243%2C243)%22%2F%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E)![BLOG-2817 2](https://blog.cloudflare.com/_image?href=https%3A%2F%2Fblog.cloudflare.com%2F_emdash%2Fapi%2Fmedia%2Ffile%2F01KW44ZVPBD39HGMEGMX3CQ0DE.png&w=715&h=402&f=webp&fit=cover&position=center)

Request traffic from users in Portugal to Cloudflare’s [1.1.1.1 DNS resolver](https://1.1.1.1/dns) also fell when the power went out, initially dropping by 40% as compared to the previous week, and falling further over the next several hours.

![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%201%201%22%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%221%22%20height%3D%221%22%20fill%3D%22rgb(243%2C243%2C243)%22%2F%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E)![BLOG-2817 3](https://blog.cloudflare.com/_image?href=https%3A%2F%2Fblog.cloudflare.com%2F_emdash%2Fapi%2Fmedia%2Ffile%2F01KW496NTS29SSNH02HJ9NK006.png&w=715&h=402&f=webp&fit=cover&position=center)

### [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/how-power-outage-in-portugal-spain-impacted-internet/#network-level) Network level
At a network level, the loss of Internet traffic from local providers including NOS, Vodafone, MEO, and NOWO was swift and significant. The Cloudflare Radar graphs below show that traffic from those networks effectively evaporated over the hours after the power outage began. However, MEO breaks out at least some of their mobile traffic onto a separate ASN, and the graph below for MEO-MOVEL (AS42863) shows that request traffic from that network more than doubled after the power went out, as subscribers turned to their mobile devices for information about what was happening. However, despite the initial spike, this mobile traffic also fell over the next several hours, dropping to approximately half of the volume seen the prior week.

![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%201%201%22%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%221%22%20height%3D%221%22%20fill%3D%22rgb(243%2C243%2C243)%22%2F%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E)![BLOG-2817 4](https://blog.cloudflare.com/_image?href=https%3A%2F%2Fblog.cloudflare.com%2F_emdash%2Fapi%2Fmedia%2Ffile%2F01KW46X6BHX7KMM6CC3W7PYJ47.png&w=715&h=402&f=webp&fit=cover&position=center)

