## [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/how-power-outage-in-portugal-spain-impacted-internet/#impacts-in-spain) Impacts in Spain
### [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/how-power-outage-in-portugal-spain-impacted-internet/#country-level-2) Country level
In Spain, Internet traffic dropped as the power grid failed, with traffic immediately dropping by around 60% as compared to the previous week, falling to approximately 80% below the previous week within the next five hours.

![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%201%201%22%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%221%22%20height%3D%221%22%20fill%3D%22rgb(243%2C243%2C243)%22%2F%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E)![BLOG-2817 29](https://blog.cloudflare.com/_image?href=https%3A%2F%2Fblog.cloudflare.com%2F_emdash%2Fapi%2Fmedia%2Ffile%2F01KW46P81VT47ECBN8DY71365S.png&w=715&h=402&f=webp&fit=cover&position=center)

Request traffic from users in Spain to Cloudflare’s [1.1.1.1 DNS resolver](https://1.1.1.1/dns) also fell when the power went out, initially dropping by 54% as compared to the previous week, but quickly stabilizing.

![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%201%201%22%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%221%22%20height%3D%221%22%20fill%3D%22rgb(243%2C243%2C243)%22%2F%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E)![BLOG-2817 30](https://blog.cloudflare.com/_image?href=https%3A%2F%2Fblog.cloudflare.com%2F_emdash%2Fapi%2Fmedia%2Ffile%2F01KW44DAKGBNRPM3CAEDV2C830.png&w=715&h=402&f=webp&fit=cover&position=center)

## [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/how-power-outage-in-portugal-spain-impacted-internet/#impacts-in-other-european-countries) Impacts in other European countries
Parts of Andorra and France were also [reportedly impacted](https://www.euronews.com/my-europe/2025/04/28/spain-portugal-and-parts-of-france-hit-by-massive-power-outage) by the power outage, with additional outages reported as far away as Belgium. At a national level, no traffic disruptions were evident in any of the countries.

Analysis of traffic at a regional level in France shows a slight decline concurrent with the power outage in several regions, but the drops were nominal in comparison to Spain and Portugal, and traffic volumes recovered to expected levels within 90 minutes.

In a [post on X](https://x.com/OrangeMaroc/status/1916866583047147690), the provider stated (translated) “ _Internet traffic has been disrupted following a massive power outage in Spain and Portugal, which is affecting international connections._” Cloudflare Radar shows that traffic from the network fell sharply around 12:00 UTC, 90 minutes after the power outage began, with a full outage beginning around 15:00 UTC.

![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%201%201%22%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%221%22%20height%3D%221%22%20fill%3D%22rgb(243%2C243%2C243)%22%2F%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E)![BLOG-2817 55](https://blog.cloudflare.com/_image?href=https%3A%2F%2Fblog.cloudflare.com%2F_emdash%2Fapi%2Fmedia%2Ffile%2F01KW47W1GN8XDVMF8B2E5EPBXP.png&w=715&h=402&f=webp&fit=cover&position=center)

## [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/how-power-outage-in-portugal-spain-impacted-internet/#conclusion) Conclusion
As power is restored, Internet traffic and other metrics will recover as well. The current state of Internet connectivity in [Spain](https://radar.cloudflare.com/es) and [Portugal](https://radar.cloudflare.com/pt) can be tracked on Cloudflare Radar.

The Cloudflare Radar team is constantly monitoring for Internet disruptions, sharing our observations on the [Cloudflare Radar Outage Center](https://radar.cloudflare.com/outage-center), via social media, and in posts on [blog.cloudflare.com](https://blog.cloudflare.com/tag/cloudflare-radar/).

