  The Internet’s reliance on a stable electric grid was made abundantly clear during the quarter, with a massive [power outage](https://blog.cloudflare.com/q2-2025-internet-disruption-summary/#power-outages-lead-to-internet-outages) impacting Spain and Portugal disrupting connectivity within those countries.

## [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/q2-2025-internet-disruption-summary/#power-outages-lead-to-internet-outages) Power outages lead to Internet outages
### [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/q2-2025-internet-disruption-summary/#portugal-spain) Portugal & Spain
The big power outage story during the second quarter was the massive outage across much of [Portugal](https://radar.cloudflare.com/traffic/pt) and [Spain](https://radar.cloudflare.com/traffic/es) on April 28. The impact of the event was covered in detail in the [_How the April 28, 2025, power outage in Portugal and Spain impacted Internet traffic and connectivity_ blog post](https://blog.cloudflare.com/how-power-outage-in-portugal-spain-impacted-internet/), which explored shifts in traffic at a country/network/regional level, as well as how the power outage impacted network quality and announced IP address space.

In Portugal, Internet traffic dropped as the power grid failed — when compared with the previous week, traffic fell ~50 % immediately and within five hours it was ~90% below the week before.

![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%201%201%22%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%221%22%20height%3D%221%22%20fill%3D%22rgb(243%2C243%2C243)%22%2F%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E)![BLOG-2817 2](https://blog.cloudflare.com/_image?href=https%3A%2F%2Fblog.cloudflare.com%2F_emdash%2Fapi%2Fmedia%2Ffile%2F01KW44ZVPBD39HGMEGMX3CQ0DE.png&w=715&h=402&f=webp&fit=cover&position=center)

In Spain, Internet traffic dropped as the power grid failed, with traffic immediately dropping by around 60% as compared to the previous week, falling to approximately 80% below the previous week within the next five hours.

![](data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%201%201%22%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%221%22%20height%3D%221%22%20fill%3D%22rgb(243%2C243%2C243)%22%2F%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E)![BLOG-2870 image 4](https://blog.cloudflare.com/_image?href=https%3A%2F%2Fblog.cloudflare.com%2F_emdash%2Fapi%2Fmedia%2Ffile%2F01KW47PK4VJAFP4R34JZM73RJQ.png&w=715&h=401&f=webp&fit=cover&position=center)

More details about the outage can be found in the blog post linked above.

### [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/q2-2025-internet-disruption-summary/#morocco) Morocco
It appears that [Morocco](https://radar.cloudflare.com/traffic/ma) may have also been impacted in some fashion by the Portugal/Spain power outage, or at least Orange Maroc was. In a [post on X](https://x.com/OrangeMaroc/status/1916866583047147690), the provider stated (translated) “ _Internet traffic has been disrupted following a massive power outage in Spain and Portugal, which is affecting international connections._” [Traffic from the network (AS36925)](https://radar.cloudflare.com/traffic/as36925?dateStart=2025-04-28&dateEnd=2025-04-29) fell sharply around 12:00 UTC (13:00 local time), 90 minutes after the power outage began, with a full outage beginning around 15:00 UTC (16:00 local time).

## [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/q2-2025-internet-disruption-summary/#technical-problems) Technical problems
### [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/q2-2025-internet-disruption-summary/#lumencenturylink) Lumen/CenturyLink
Traffic volumes dropped by over 50% as compared to the prior week starting around 21:45 UTC.

Problems with an Internet service provider’s DNS resolver can appear to subscribers like an Internet outage, as they become unable to access anything requiring a DNS lookup (effectively, all Internet resources), ultimately resulting in a drop in traffic to those resources (from the affected user base), as seen in the graph above.

## [Copy link](https://blog.cloudflare.com/q2-2025-internet-disruption-summary/#conclusion) Conclusion
And while power-outage related Internet disruptions have frequently been observed in the past, often in smaller countries with less stable infrastructure, the massive outage in Spain and Portugal on April 28 reminds us that much like the Internet, electrical infrastructure is often interconnected across countries, meaning that problems in one can potentially cause significant problems in others.

